@participant2 No, I have never used Grafana Live. Apart from normal Grafana's panels & a few plugins, I only have small script that gets data from specific URL and shows it in the text panel.
```
<html>
<body>
	<div style="display:flex; width:100%; height:100%; align-items:start; justify-content: center;">
		<div>
			<p style="font-size:18px; text-align:center; font-family:Roboto,Helvetica Neue,Arial,sans-serif; font-weight:500; color:#d8d9da;" id="sager"></p>
		</div>
	</div>
  <script>
  
	var forecastDone;
  
    document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function(){
		}); {
		timeInterval = setInterval(checkTime, 10000);
		getData();
		};
    
		function checkTime() {
			var date = new Date();
			if((date.getMinutes() % 10 == 0) && (date.getSeconds() > 10) && (forecastDone == 0)) {
				getData();
			}
			else if((date.getMinutes() % 10 != 0) && (forecastDone == 1)) {
				forecastDone = 0;
			}
		}
	
		function getData() {
			var url = 'https://stacjameteommz.pl/forecasts/minsk_mazowiecki_1.txt';
			fetch(url)
				.then(function(response) {
					response.text().then(function(text) {
						var t = document.getElementById("sager");
						t.textContent = text;
						forecastDone = 1;
					});
				});
		}
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

EDIT: I have checked that Grafana Live is enabled by default. I have never disabled it either, so I guess it is enabled on my server.

I have also noticed 500 error twice today on Windows and Google Chrome. It started happening after my yesterday's changes, but I believe it is not related to iOS issue in specific. I think it may occur because I have enabled `multi_accept` yesterday. I have just disabled it again to see if it'll help.

Here is my current nginx config:
```
events {
        worker_connections 2048;
        #multi_accept on;
}

http {
        [...]

        map $http_upgrade $connection_upgrade {
                default upgrade;
                '' close;
        }

        server {
                listen 443 ssl;
                ssl_certificate [cert_file-censored];
                ssl_certificate_key [key_file-censored];
                server_name stacjameteommz.pl www.stacjameteommz.pl;
                keepalive_timeout 60;

                location / {
                        proxy_http_version 1.1;
                        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
                        proxy_set_header Connection $connection_upgrade;
                        proxy_set_header Host $host;
                        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;

                        proxy_pass https://localhost:30016/;
                }
                location = / {
                        return 301 https://stacjameteommz.pl/d/1/stacja-meteorologiczna-minsk-mazowiecki?kiosk=tv;
                }
                location /ads.txt {
                        root /usr/share/grafana/public;
                }
                location /app-ads.txt {
                        root /usr/share/grafana/public;
                }
                location /app_privacy_policy.html {
                        root /usr/share/grafana/public;
                }
                location /forecasts/ {
                        root /usr/share/grafana/public;
                }
        }

        server {
                listen 80;
                server_name stacjameteommz.pl www.stacjameteommz.pl;

                return 301 https://stacjameteommz.pl$request_uri;
        }
}
```

@participant4 Thank you for the suggestions. Have you checked if it works on your Grafana instance with SSL turned on? May it be the problem related to using Grafana with SSL?

On my server I think there are only nginx and Grafana that may cause that issue. Other things that I have on the server are not related (InfluxDB, Telegraf, some Python scripts that run locally). I don't use any load balancer. Do you find something unusual in my nginx config that I've posted above?
Maybe the problem is that I use Grafana on non-standard port?

@participant7 Thank you for checking my case.
I have changed my Google Tag Manager script to set the viewport tag to `content="width=1024"`, as I want to force the desktop Grafana view on mobiles.
Unfortunately, it seems that is is not working either on iOS. I have checked it only on the online iOS device, which limits the session length to 1 minute. Could you see it by yourself to check what's causing the issue now?

I've found the source of the issue. Other script from my Google Tag Manager is freezing the page on iOS/macOS. The issue is with this line:
```mutation.target.textContent = "new_title"```

in script:
```
<script>
  var target = document.querySelector('head > title');
  var observer = new window.WebKitMutationObserver(function(mutations) {
    mutations.forEach(function(mutation) {
      mutation.target.textContent = "new_title"
    });
  });
  observer.observe(target, { subtree: true, characterData: true, childList: true });
</script>
```

That's a bit strange, because MutationObservers are supported in Safari. However, I've changed the way to change page's title and now it is working well on both iOS and macOS.

Should we close the issue now, as other people in this issue had commented about similar issue that may be caused by Grafana itself?
